{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "3. Extension Points: Modularity & Pluggability",
  "description": "Where collaborators can plug in their own modules (a new backend adapter, a new TTS engine), how the two real extension points are shaped, and — just as important — which modules are deliberately NOT pluggable and why, so effort isn't wasted fighting the existing design. Companion to '1. Architecture & Data Flow'.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Start here",
      "description": "**Only two things in this codebase are actually designed to be pluggable: the backend adapter and the TTS engine.** Everything else — audio capture/playback, VAD, STT, safeword detection — is a single concrete class with no interface, on purpose.\n\nThat's a deliberate, hard-won design choice, not an oversight: an earlier related project (`voice-opencode`) built a multi-engine STT/TTS factory with cloud fallback *before* a single local engine actually worked end to end — pluggability was built before there was anything real to abstract over. See `README.md` → \"Lessons from an earlier attempt\" for the full story.\n\nSo: this tour shows you exactly where to plug in, using the two `ABC`s that exist specifically because multiple implementations were anticipated — and calls out where NOT to add one."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/convobox/adapters/base.py",
      "line": 32,
      "pattern": "class BackendAdapter\\(ABC\\):",
      "description": "**Extension point 1: `BackendAdapter`.** One implementation per target CLI. Three exist today, each verified against a live instance: `OpenCodeAdapter` (HTTP+SSE), `ClaudeCodeAdapter` (bidirectional stream-json subprocess), `CodexAdapter` (app-server JSON-RPC over stdio) -- a fourth is where YOU come in.\n\nThe contract is 5 methods: `send_text`, `send_interject`, `send_hard_stop`, `is_busy`, `events`. Read the class docstring here carefully before implementing a new one — it's not just a method list, it draws hard semantic lines (interject vs. hard-stop must never collapse into one \"interrupt\" concept) and an honesty clause about what `send_hard_stop` can actually guarantee if the target backend has no real cancel endpoint.\n\n**Correction (see `OPENCODE_API_NOTES.md`):** the concrete example that honesty clause points to (`OpenCodeAdapter`, next step) turned out to be wrong about OpenCode having no cancel endpoint — a real one exists, the adapter just wasn't built against the real API. The general principle in this docstring still holds; the specific example doesn't. Each of the three adapters' own module docstrings (`opencode.py`, `claude_code.py`, `codex.py`) documents its own live-probed protocol quirks the same honest way -- read the one closest to whatever you're building against before assuming a shape."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/convobox/adapters/opencode.py",
      "line": 27,
      "pattern": "class OpenCodeAdapter\\(BackendAdapter\\):",
      "description": "**A reference implementation — but a wrong one. Read `OPENCODE_API_NOTES.md` before copying this pattern.** `OpenCodeAdapter` targets `POST /api/sessions`, `GET /api/sessions/:id/events`, `POST /api/sessions/:id/messages` — inferred from an earlier related project's documentation, never verified against a real server. Testing against an actual `opencode serve` instance (2026-07-10) found the real API uses different, singular paths (`/session`, `/session/:id/message`, `/session/:id/event`), a completely different message body shape (a `parts` array, not `{messages: [...]}`), a real `/session/:id/abort` endpoint this code claims doesn't exist, and a much richer ~28-type event model instead of this code's flat 5-value enum.\n\n**The shape of the pattern is still right** — thin typed client, `_ensure_session`/`_post_message`/`events()` as the structure, `warn_if_insecure` for network backends — copy that. Just don't trust this file's actual endpoint paths or body shapes as ground truth; verify against whatever real API your new adapter targets, the way this investigation verified (and corrected) this one."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/convobox/config.py",
      "line": 39,
      "pattern": "class BackendConfig\\(BaseModel\\):",
      "description": "**The config-side selector — now wired.** `BackendConfig.name` (default \"opencode\") selects which adapter `create_backend_adapter()` (in `adapters/__init__.py`) constructs — `OpenCodeAdapter` today, raising a clear `ValueError` for anything else rather than silently doing nothing. If you're adding a second adapter, add its branch there."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/convobox/tts/base.py",
      "line": 24,
      "pattern": "class TTSEngine\\(ABC\\):",
      "description": "**Extension point 2: `TTSEngine`.** Two real implementations exist today, exactly the state Tour 3's own selection rule (see \"Start here\") calls for an ABC: **Kokoro** (`kokoro.py`, the **default engine** since 2026-07-24, PR #141 — MIT wrapper, Apache-2.0 model weights) and **Piper** (`piper.py`, GPL-3.0, opt-in via `--extra piper` for exactly that licensing reason — see `DEPENDENCY_LICENSE_AUDIT.md`).\n\nThe contract: `sample_rate` (property), `synthesize_stream` (the one concrete engines must implement), `stop`, `is_speaking`. Read the docstring here too — it states a hard requirement: **every concrete engine must call `sanitize_text` before synthesizing**, since the input text originates from untrusted LLM output. That's not optional style guidance, it's the security boundary this whole TTS layer exists to enforce (see the '2. Review Findings' tour for why)."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/convobox/tts/kokoro.py",
      "line": 34,
      "pattern": "class KokoroTTSEngine\\(TTSEngine\\):",
      "description": "**The default engine, and the second implementation that justified the ABC.** `KokoroTTSEngine` loads the shared ONNX model/voice bundle once at construction (`model_path`/`voices_path`, resolved against `.models/kokoro/`) and implements `synthesize_stream` as a true async generator, same shape `PiperTTSEngine` uses (see the next step) — proof the `TTSEngine` contract generalizes across a genuinely different underlying library, not just a hypothetical second engine.\n\nWorth knowing if you're chasing audio quality here: Kokoro has a hard **~510-phoneme synthesis limit** per call — not a config knob, a real model constraint. See `docs/KNOWN-ISSUES.md`'s Speech pipeline section."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/convobox/tts/piper.py",
      "line": 16,
      "pattern": "class PiperTTSEngine\\(TTSEngine\\):",
      "description": "**The GPL-licensed opt-in alternative — same contract shape as Kokoro.** Notice the shape a *third* engine would need to match: load whatever voice/model once at construction, expose `sample_rate` from it, and implement `synthesize_stream` as a true async generator — bridging a blocking/sync synthesis call through a background thread + queue if the underlying library isn't natively async (see `synthesize_stream` a bit further down, and the '1. Architecture & Data Flow' tour for why that bridge pattern matters for time-to-first-audio)."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/convobox/config.py",
      "line": 29,
      "pattern": "class TTSConfig\\(BaseModel\\):",
      "description": "**Same selector, now wired on the TTS side too.** `TTSConfig.engine` (default **\"kokoro\"**) selects which engine `create_tts_engine()` (in `tts/factory.py`) constructs. For Piper, `TTSConfig.voice` is a voice **key** (e.g. `en_GB-alba-medium`), not a raw file path -- the factory resolves it to the actual `model_path`/`config_path` pair via `resolve_voice_paths()` against Piper's downloaded-voices directory; Kokoro instead reads `model_path`/`voices_path`/`language` directly off this same config object. This is deliberate: the config surface stays engine-agnostic where it can (a key a human picks with `scripts/voice_picker.py`), while each engine's own file-path resolution stays an internal factory concern the other engine doesn't need to know about."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/convobox/orchestrator/orchestrator.py",
      "line": 30,
      "pattern": "tts: TTSEngine \\| None = None,",
      "description": "**The extension point that actually works today, proven by tests.** `Orchestrator` takes `tts`/`player` as optional constructor arguments — plain dependency injection, no factory needed here. Anything satisfying the `TTSEngine` ABC (or, for `player`, a real `AudioPlayer` subclass — see the next step for why that one isn't an ABC) can be passed in without touching a single line of `Orchestrator`'s internals.\n\nThis is exactly how the test suite substitutes fakes for real audio hardware — not a theoretical claim, see the next step."
    },
    {
      "file": "tests/test_orchestrator.py",
      "line": 40,
      "pattern": "class FakeTTSEngine\\(TTSEngine\\):",
      "description": "**Proof the injection point works.** `FakeTTSEngine` is a real subclass of the `TTSEngine` ABC (it has to be — mypy rejects a duck-typed stand-in, see `TESTING.md` → Type checking) substituted straight into `Orchestrator`'s constructor in place of `PiperTTSEngine`. No real audio hardware, no model downloads, and the orchestrator's routing/speaking logic is exercised exactly as it would be with a real engine.\n\n`FakePlayer` a few lines down does the same for `AudioPlayer` — but subclasses the concrete class rather than an ABC, since `AudioPlayer` has none (see the next two steps for why that's deliberate, not a gap to fix)."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/convobox/audio/capture.py",
      "line": 21,
      "pattern": "class MicrophoneStream:",
      "description": "**What's deliberately NOT pluggable, part 1.** `MicrophoneStream` (and `AudioPlayer`, `UtteranceSegmenter`, `LocalTranscriber`, `SafewordDetector` — none of them) has no ABC, no factory, no \"engine\" config field. Each is a single concrete class doing one job.\n\nThis is a design choice, not a TODO: this codebase currently needs exactly one working implementation per role, and building a plugin abstraction for a role with only one real implementation adds real complexity (an interface to maintain, a factory to keep in sync, a config schema to reconcile) for zero present benefit. If you have a second real, working implementation of one of these (a different VAD library, a Windows-native audio backend, etc.), that's the moment to introduce an ABC here — not before."
    },
    {
      "title": "Adding a new adapter or TTS engine: checklist",
      "description": "**New `BackendAdapter`** (OpenCode, Claude Code, and Codex all exist now -- this is for a fourth):\n1. Subclass `BackendAdapter`, implement all 5 methods against that tool's actual headless/structured interface.\n2. Be honest in `send_hard_stop`'s docstring about what it can/can't guarantee — don't copy `OpenCodeAdapter`'s ORIGINAL claim that no cancel endpoint exists as a pattern; that claim was itself wrong for OpenCode (see `OPENCODE_API_NOTES.md`). Verify against the real target API instead of inferring from docs -- `claude_code.py`'s and `codex.py`'s module docstrings show what that live-probing discipline looks like for a stream-json subprocess and a JSON-RPC-over-stdio server respectively, two shapes quite different from OpenCode's HTTP+SSE.\n3. Add a branch to `create_backend_adapter()` (`adapters/__init__.py`) -- the `BackendConfig.name -> AdapterClass` factory already exists, covering all three current adapters.\n4. Also consider `force_kill()` (see the '2. Review Findings' tour's kill-phrase step) -- the base class default is a reasonable no-op-beyond-`aclose()`, but if your target CLI spawns real subprocess children the way codex does, decide up front whether reaching them needs the same kind of platform-specific escalation codex.py required, rather than discovering it live.\n5. Test against a real server/process the way `tests/test_opencode_adapter.py` (real loopback socket), `tests/test_claude_code_adapter.py` (fake subprocess speaking real stream-json), and `tests/test_codex_adapter.py` (fake subprocess speaking real JSON-RPC) each do — not just mocks of your own client, and ideally against the real running service too, not just a fake server that mirrors your own assumptions (that's exactly how `OpenCodeAdapter`'s wrong API shape went undetected for as long as it did).\n\n**New `TTSEngine`** (a third, alongside Kokoro and Piper):\n1. Subclass `TTSEngine`, implement `sample_rate` and `synthesize_stream`.\n2. Call `sanitize_text` on the input **before** synthesizing — non-negotiable, see step 5 of this tour.\n3. If the underlying library's synthesis call is blocking/sync, bridge it through a background thread + queue the way `PiperTTSEngine`/`KokoroTTSEngine` do — don't buffer the whole response before yielding anything (see the '2. Review Findings' tour for why that was a real, measured regression when it happened here).\n4. Test with a fake voice object (see `tests/test_tts.py`'s `_FakeVoice`) — no real model download needed for unit tests.\n\nFor either: run `pytest tests/`, `ruff check`, and `mypy src/convobox` (see `TESTING.md`) before considering it done."
    }
  ]
}
